ource-plugins.md", ".prawduct/changes/audit-wave-2b.md", ".prawduct/operator-verification.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "warning", "title": "Contract 1.3.0's 32-output cap narrows a minor, but the governed_by disposition and the release entry call the minor additive except for one corrected answer", "recommendation": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation, player-contract norm 'a minor is additive, a released tag is not moved'. The disposition says 'conforms ... one answer is corrected, not added (47's not_an_object row)', and player-contract.md \u00a7 Releases says 1.3.0 is 'Additive but for one corrected answer'. The diff makes a second answer non-additive. `semantic_errors` in contract/src/arrt_contract/__init__.py now refuses a client heartbeat with more than MAX_OUTPUTS_PER_REPORT (32) outputs or label outputs, and so does the server (programming/client_heartbeat.py `problem_with`, since Chunk 40, commit e3c34db2). A document of that size passed 1.2.0's schema and its semantic rules, and it fit the 256 KiB body cap. The only rule that existed before was the duplicate-name rule. Because 1.3.0 is not cut, the server at this merge refuses documents that are valid under the latest released contract, 1.2.0, which is what the Player pins. The fixture check (test_released) does not catch this: no 1.2.0 valid fixture has more than 32 outputs. The practical risk is close to nil, because no Player sends more than a handful. But the record tells the Player's coordinator that only one answer changed, and the plan holds no DECISION that weighs this narrowing against the additive norm. Chunk 60 lists it as a deliverable, with no cost and no ruling. Fix (instance): add a mid-build DECISION in the plan's form naming the narrowing and why it is safe (the body cap, the Players' real counts, a DoS bound on rows). Then make the disposition and the 1.3.0 release line say there are two non-additive changes, not one.", "files": ["contract/src/arrt_contract/__init__.py", "arrt/src/arrt/programming/client_heartbeat.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The no-paid-call guard reads only *.yml workflows, so a model key in a .yaml workflow passes it", "recommendation": "tests/test_ci_makes_no_paid_call.py `_workflows()` returns `WORKFLOWS.glob(\"*.yml\")`, but GitHub also runs `.github/workflows/*.yaml`. The module docstring promises it is 'Read from every file under `.github/workflows/`'. This test is the only enforcer of the owner's ruling of 2026-11-20 (no workflow holds a model key). A `nightly.yaml` holding OPENROUTER_API_KEY would leave it green, and so would test_every_secret_ci_may_hold_is_one_a_workflow_reads. The repo's own sibling guards already read both extensions: test_actions_pinned.py:48, test_one_python.py:38, test_default_suite_ci_scope.py:303 and test_assert_tests_ran.py:319. Learnings rule: 'When a safety check guards a filtered feature, range it over the population the hazard lives in'. Fix (class, bounded): glob `*.y*ml` here, and add a case that seeds a `.yaml` workflow carrying a secret and asserts it is found. The same narrower glob is in test_actions_pinned.py:159 (`test_no_job_that_holds_a_secret_can_write_issues`), a member of the same class that this diff touched. Grep tests/ for `glob(\"*.yml\")` to find any others.", "files": ["tests/test_ci_makes_no_paid_call.py", "tests/test_actions_pinned.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The published plugin-testing helper keeps its no-network promise by overriding two private methods of AcquisitionService, and its tiled override has no test", "recommendation": "Instance. `arrt.library.sources.testing` is a supported, versioned API for plugin authors, and it promises it 'fetches nothing and reaches no network'. It keeps that promise through `_StopsBeforeFetching`, a subclass of `AcquisitionService` that overrides the private `_acquire_tiled` and `_acquire_direct`, and it passes a duck-typed `_Ledger` in through `cast(\"CatalogueService\", ...)`. Why it broke: the helper was built on the service's private seam because the service exposes no fetch seam of its own for tiles. The direct path is fenced: `open_stream=_nowhere` raises if the override is ever bypassed. The tiled path has no fence. If someone renames or splits `_acquire_tiled` (it is private, so they will assume nothing outside the class uses it), `read()` on a reader that answers `LocatorKind.TILES` falls through to the real `tile_fetch` and runs the `dezoomify-rs` subprocess inside a plugin author's test suite. IIIF museums answer TILES most of the time. No test in `arrt/tests/unit/test_plugin_testing_helper.py` covers a tiles answer: every `WouldFetch` assertion there is `\"direct\"`. Fix: inject the tile fetch the way `open_stream` is already injected (a `tile_fetch` callable on `AcquisitionService`, defaulting to the real one), and have the helper pass a stopper for both paths instead of subclassing private methods. Then add a tiles-answer test for `read()`. If the subclass stays, at minimum add that test and a stand-in `tile_binary` that fails loudly.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/sources/testing.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/service.py", "arrt/tests/unit/test_plugin_testing_helper.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "HealthService.observe_waiting has no caller and is the shape of the double read Chunk 66 removed", "recommendation": "Instance: delete `HealthService.observe_waiting` (arrt/src/arrt/services/health.py). Nothing in `arrt/src` or `arrt/tests` calls it. Every walls answer now calls `observe_walls()`, so that a wall's state and its wait come from one read (`WallsNow`). Why it is still there: it predates `observe_walls`, which took over its job and left it in place. As long as it stays public, the obvious way to add 'whom does this wall wait for' to an answer is to call it next to a separate `survey_wall_status()`. That is exactly the read-twice race this wave fixed, where a first report landing between the two reads makes an answer say a wall waits while its badge shows a work. Removing the method leaves only the one-read entry point.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/services/health.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "New server sentences hand-roll count agreement and digit grouping beside arrt.counting", "recommendation": "Class, bounded by `grep -nE '== 1 else' arrt/src/arrt` (17 sites today). This diff adds about 8 of them: `services/health.py` lines 252 and 480 (\"1 work\" if ... else f\"{count:,} works\"), the 'it is'/'they are', 'has'/'have' and 'waits'/'wait' choices in `HealthReading.describe`, `_player_words`, `_setup_words` and `_waiting_words`, plus `library/sources/loading.py` (the held-back journal line) and `library/discovery/pool.py` ('it.'/'them.'). `health.py` already imports `arrt.counting.counted`, and `counting.py`'s docstring states the rule these sites break: copying the agreement per surface is how it got right in one place and wrong in another. They also disagree on format. `counted(2000, \"work\")` writes '2000 works' and the new trouble writes '2,000 works', so the server now writes a count of thousands two ways. Why: `counted` has no grouping, so new code that wanted grouping wrote its own. Construction: give `counted` the grouping (or a `grouped=` flag) and route the new sites through `counted` and `agree`. That leaves one owner every count passes through, and the next correction lands everywhere.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/services/health.py", "arrt/src/arrt/counting.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/sources/loading.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/discovery/pool.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A slow source's owed asks now overlap without bound: the 30 s answer window lets every work start a new call at a plugin still answering the last", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. It broke because Chunk 33 stopped the run waiting for the slowest source, and nothing replaced the one-call-per-source-at-a-time that waiting gave. In arrt/src/arrt/library/discovery/pool.py, `_start` starts a daemon thread per source for every work, and `_stop_waiting` abandons a source still asking at ANSWER_WINDOW_SECONDS (30 s) as owed while its call runs on to the plugin's own deadline. `_Calls` in arrt/src/arrt/library/sources/loading.py bounds only calls past their deadline (STRAGGLERS_PER_PLUGIN = 4). It does not bound owed calls still inside it, and `_may_answer`/`_owed_by` look only at this work's record, not at other works' asks to the same provider. So while phase 2 or the queue goes from work to work, one plugin that takes D seconds gets about D/30 calls at once. For the private browser plugins whose declared bounds the loader's comment gives (1,012 s a page, 2,044 s a holder read), that is roughly 34 to 68 concurrent calls at a museum behind a crawl delay, plus two threads each on the NAS. Before 2b it was one. The Chunk 33 DECISION's recorded cost ('a work may settle on fewer sources than will answer') does not name this, and nothing logs or shows how many asks are in flight. The fix: bound in-flight asks per provider, owed ones included. For example, a provider that already has an owed ask in flight is answered as owed or not-asked for the next work without being started, and that is counted in the search record. Then record the cost in the plan's DECISION. The same edit should delete the now-false clause in loading.py's DEADLINE_CEILING_SECONDS comment ('because a run waits for every source'). The learnings rule 'When a comment justifies code by a constraint, or names what a guard excludes, check the constraint' applies to that comment.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/discovery/pool.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/sources/loading.py"], "fid": "R-6"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 6, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Two governed_by dispositions carry stale sub-claims about which chunks touched what, though their 'conforms' verdicts hold", "recommendation": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation. The architecture disposition says '45 and 53 are the only chunks that edit arrt/src/arrt/mcp/', but chunks 60, 62, 64 and 66 also commit there (git log over mcp/). The player-contract disposition says '47 edits player-contract.md \u00a7 Transport, 54 writes \u00a7 Releases' 1.3.0 entry; no other chunk edits it', but 58, 59 and 60 also edit it. I checked the substance and it holds. The new MCP bindings code only maps service values onto fields and slices pages. No new Library-to-Programming or Programming-to-Library import was added. LibraryFacade is unchanged. The contract edits follow the package's rules, apart from the cap in the finding above. Only the enumerations are stale. If the disposition text is touched for that finding, make these claims relational, or drop the chunk lists.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-audit-wave-2b.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "dezoomify's disk-fault words are keyed by hand against atomic.DISK_FAULT_ERRNOS", "recommendation": "`tile_fetch` raises `OSError(disk, f\"{_DISK_FAULT_WORDS[disk]}: ...\")` for any errno in `atomic.DISK_FAULT_ERRNOS`. `_DISK_FAULT_WORDS` covers the set's two members (ENOSPC, EROFS) by hand. If an errno is added to the set (EDQUOT is the likely one), the full-disk path raises KeyError instead of the OSError the queue pauses on, and the fault is counted against the work. Learnings: 'When a comment names a symbol as the source of truth for a set, parametrise the test over that symbol'. An import-time check (set(DISK_FAULT_ERRNOS) <= set(_DISK_FAULT_WORDS)), or a test parametrised over the set, closes it. Latent today.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/dezoomify.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Evidence and probes", "recommendation": "test-status: current, and tree-valid on aef0c373 (the dispatch tree): 11,243 passed, 0 failed, 5 skipped, under `testament run`. verify-coverage: skipped (coverage_required false). I checked the 93 test functions the diff removes by name. All that I sampled are renames with sharper names, or retirements the record documents: the paid drift tier (owner ruling, Chunk 66), the image path filter (Chunk 56), Remove\u2192Forget (41), interface 1.5\u21921.6 and the MCP break (45). No unrecorded assertion weakening was found. No new dependency is added in any pyproject.toml.", "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Shipped comments carry chunk numbers, review ids and history narration (about 30 added in this diff)", "recommendation": "Class, bounded by grepping the diff's added lines for `[Cc]hunk [0-9]+`, `R-[0-9]+` and 'boundary review': there are about 30 new ones across `arrt/src` (for example lookups.py's 'Until 2026-11-20 they were methods of FeedPublisher ... (the 2a boundary review, R-3)', health.py's '(wave 2b, Chunk 66, found at integration)', walls.js's '(`waiting_for`, wave 2b Chunk 62)', and models.py's per-field '(wave 2b, Chunk 32)'). Chunk numbers are ephemeral build ids: this wave already moved chunks 38 and 46 out, and the plan gets archived. The remedy is deletion, not rewording. This is comment wording, so it is not a finding, and it fits the deferred docstring sweep (draft 46). The sweep should take this grep as its input, and the next chunk touching these modules should not add more.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/programming/lookups.py", "arrt/src/arrt/services/health.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/models.py", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/walls.js"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "failure_instant inverts the queue's retry schedule from outside the queue", "recommendation": "`services/health.py` `failure_instant` works out a legacy row's failure instant as `next_try_at - RETRY_AFTER[failures - 1]`. That is the inverse of a formula that lives in `AcquisitionQueue._record_failure`, so the health module now knows how the queue schedules. It affects only rows written before `failed_at` existed, and those decay as works are retried, so the stakes are small. If the schedule ever changes shape (jitter, a cap), the ages shown for those rows go wrong silently. It would sit better as a queue-owned function next to `_record_failure`.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/services/health.py", "arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/queue.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: every top-bar health read scans every owed work to count the failed ones", "recommendation": "`AcquisitionQueue.failed_states` (feeding `HealthService.observe_failed_fetches`, which `GET /api/health` and so the top bar read on every page) iterates `works_to_acquire()`, every work owed an image, and runs `_wait`/`_state` on each to keep the failed ones. The builder already cut it to one read per work and measured it at 2,000 failed works, so it is a known, bounded cost today. It still grows with the owed set rather than with what the top bar uses, which is a count and one maximum. If that set grows, a store-side count of rows with failures plus the newest `failed_at` would make it constant. No edit is wanted now. Record the volume under `templates/data-model.md` 'Access Paths' if one is not stated.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/library/acquisition/queue.py", "arrt/src/arrt/services/health.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Framework-Specific Checks: only CLAUDE.md among instruction files changed, and it reads clearly", "recommendation": "No skill or template file is in the diff. The CLAUDE.md change (three projects, six pool suites, the two client guards to run after touching static/) checks out against `.testament.toml`: governance, curation, browser, player, contract and image are all there, and both cited test files exist. CLAUDE.md is 112 lines, under the ~150 ceiling. Nothing to do.", "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A wall whose feed would exceed FEED_MAX_BYTES stops rolling forward, and only a one-time log line says so", "recommendation": "When a heartbeat roll or the scheduled reconcile is refused with FeedTooLarge, programming/feeds.py swallows it and logs `feed.over_bound` once per wall. The wall then keeps an old feed until its horizon runs out, and Status and the health model raise no trouble. The bound is 16 MiB, so this is unlikely at a household's size. A `troubles` entry (about `wall`) for a wall in `_over_bound` would give it a visible signal. No edit is required for this review.", "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "scope-trace: SourceSearch.images is written and nothing reads it", "recommendation": "The persistence/discovery_records.py docstring lists 'what each source contributed (`images`)' among the four questions the record answers, but no reader of `images` exists. The change record already says the tally does not divide by it and lists the remainder of #313 for the third part, so this is disclosed. Delete that question from the docstring's list, or say it is not yet answered, so the next reader does not take the yields question as built.", "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The plan still marks '/api has no reader but the browser' as inferred after Chunk 54 checked it", "recommendation": "build-plan-audit-wave-2b.md \u00a7 Goals 'Inferred, not ruled' still lists the /api inference. The change record now says api-contract.md forbids any outside binding, which settles it. The pool-runners-reach-GitHub inference stays open, with its remedy (a label) recorded. No edit is wanted: the plan is a record of the work.", "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "title": "Records Pass: operator-verification calls '1 step to finish' a visible badge,